Z||000000||mope||07-02-2001||10:06 AM||||Just put a new compressor in, evauated the system, put in new oil and r134a.<P>On my way to a swap meet yesterday i kept hearing sort of grinding noises. they were sort of quiet though. I shut off the air to keep it quoiet so i could listen for it again and didnt hear it. I then turned the air on and there it was. A nasty grinding sound. What went wrong? Any help would be good. Thanks<P>mope<P>p.s. does anyone have a black leather GT steering wheel they wanna sell me???? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||07-02-2001||11:39 AM||||My car was doing the same thing. It turned out to be a loose mounting bolt.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||07-02-2001||11:51 AM||||Ouch!  I just put a new compressor on mine and I am in the process of converting to r134a.  Did you fill the compressor with ester oil and then rotate it by hand at least 10 times?  The instruction on my rebuilt said to do this otherwise breakage is possible.  Also, did you blow dry air through all your lines, evap and condensor and clean the filter tube prior to assembly?  I found a s#*$ load of metal shavings in my system.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-02-2001||07:54 PM||||It's entirely posible you toasted the new compressor. You could have something scrubing against the clutch...<P>A company called Airsept makes a screen to help protect replacement compressors. you can get it thru Ontool.com The screen is ment to catch particles a flush may have missed. All it takes is one chunk to break the compressor.||||reg||1
